WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:07.080
Adding the "true story" tagline is a common
way to entice moviegoers to the box office.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.870
But as we all know, Hollywood has a habit
of sensationalizing things and twisting the

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.200
truth in order to keep audiences captivated.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:22.520
It's time to look past the silver screen and
see why being "based on a true story" isn't

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.690
all it's cracked up to be.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.690
Fargo

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.940
The movie starts with text that says "THIS
IS A TRUE STORY," the movie Fargo adds that

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.070
the events of the film happened in Minnesota
in 1987.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:41.370
So then why do the movie's closing credits
feature the "all persons fictitious" disclaimer?

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.530
The Coen brothers loosely based the script
on actual events and formulated the events

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.670
before and after it.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:56.149
In 2016, 20 years after the movie's 1996 debut,
the brothers finally revealed their inspirations

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:59.030
in an interview with The Huffington Post.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.030
Said Joel:

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.920
"There are actually two little elements in
the story that were based on actual incidents.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.299
One of them is the fact that there was a guy,
I believe in the ‘60s or ‘70s, who was

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:15.360
gumming up serial numbers for cars and defrauding
the General Motors Finance Corporation.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.720
There was no kidnapping.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.760
There was no murder.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.850
It was a guy defrauding the GM Finance Corporation
at some point.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.260
"The other thing [that's] based on something
real: There was a murder in Connecticut, where

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.220
a man killed his wife and disposed of the
body — put her into a wood chipper.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.680
But beyond that, the story is made up."

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:34.680
Rudy

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.390
A sports classic, Rudy is inspiring, uplifting...and
fast and loose with the truth.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.939
Remember that scene where the players place
their jerseys on Coach Devine's desk to convince

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.380
him to let Rudy play?

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.500
And the crowd chanting his name?

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.500
"Rudy!

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.500
Rudy!

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.500
Rudy!"

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.270
That never happened, and Joe Montana—who
was on the team with Rudy—confirmed to Sports

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.579
Illustrated that:

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:02.430
"It's a movie, remember.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.090
Not all that's true.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.800
The crowd wasn't chanting, nobody threw in
their jerseys.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.270
He did get in the game.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.330
He got carried off the game."

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.409
Argo

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:17.079
Ben Affleck won three Academy Awards, including
Best Picture, for Argo.

00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:21.959
But it's pretty inaccurate, especially the
way it minimizes Canada's role in the whole

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:24.459
hostage-extraction plan.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:30.090
None other than President Jimmy Carter himself
called out Argo for making stuff up.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:37.379
"My judgment is that 90% of the credit for
that heroic and brilliant move should have

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:45.669
been with the Canadians, and the movie ignores
practically any contribution by the Canadians."

00:02:45.669 --> 00:02:47.059
Foxcatcher

00:02:47.059 --> 00:02:51.949
Depicting John du Pont's murder of Olympic
wrestler Dave Schultz, plenty of what appears

00:02:51.949 --> 00:02:55.180
on screen in Foxcatcher is accurate.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.810
But the filmmakers took lots of liberties
with the timeline and characters' relationships.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.290
For instance, Foxcatcher shows du Pont's quick
apprehension by the police.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:10.639
In reality, the millionaire hid in his mansion's
secure room in a multi-day standoff.

00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:15.169
The movie also doesn't really show how much
time passes between Dave Schultz joining Team

00:03:15.169 --> 00:03:19.170
Foxcatcher, which happened in 1989, and his
death.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:24.159
Around 1996, du Pont started losing interest
in the sport as he announced he was ending

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:30.480
his $400,000 annual contributions to the team—which
wasn't featured in the film—months before

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.559
the murder.

00:03:31.559 --> 00:03:33.290
American Sniper

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.510
Critics have pointed out differences between
the life of Chris Kyle—the Navy Seal with

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.170
the most confirmed kills in United States
military history—and the character that's

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:46.339
based on him, portrayed by Bradley Cooper
in American Sniper.

00:03:46.339 --> 00:03:52.089
One of many distortions comes in the form
of Mustafa, the enemy sniper Kyle battles.

00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:57.260
Mustafa only shows up in Kyle's memoirs for
one paragraph, whereas the movie portrays

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.199
him as a longtime antagonist.

00:03:59.199 --> 00:04:06.079
In the book, Kyle's super-long-range shot
of over 2,000 yards wasn't fired on Mustafa,

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.979
but on someone carrying a rocket launcher.

00:04:08.979 --> 00:04:11.199
The Blind Side

00:04:11.199 --> 00:04:16.430
In 2009, audiences saw how Michael Oher was
adopted by a wealthy family and overcame the

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.639
odds to become an NFL star in The Blind Side.

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:24.310
It also inspired Oher to write a book to set
the record straight.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:29.810
For starters, Oher said Leigh Anne Tuhoy,
played by Sandra Bullock in the movie, didn't

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.530
teach him how to play football.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:32.530
Oher wrote:

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.110
"I felt like it portrayed me as dumb.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:39.640
Quinton Aaron did a great job acting the part,
but I could not figure out why the director

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.550
chose to show me as someone who had to be
taught the game of football."

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.569
Rush

00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:50.760
The Formula One flick Rush generated some
great reviews—but it's full of historical

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.240
exaggerations.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.949
One of the biggest distortions is the fact
that James Hunt and Niki Lauda, played by

00:04:56.949 --> 00:05:01.020
Chris Hemsworth and Daniel Bruhl, are portrayed
as bitter rivals.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.639
They were actually good friends, having once
been roommates and drinking buddies.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:10.810
Also, a few of the film's racing events were
changed as to how they turned out and who

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.810
participated.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.860
And James Hunt has also never punched a journalist.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:18.180
(James Hunt punching a journalist)

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.400
Captain Phillips

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:25.840
Based on the true story of the 2009 Maersk
Alabama hijacking by Somali pirates, Captain

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.319
Phillips shows audiences how the guy with
his name in the title saved the lives of his

00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:32.319
crew.

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:37.330
But an anonymous crewmember from the actual
ship leveled accusations that the real Phillips

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.400
isn't much of a hero at all.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:45.610
According to his claims, Phillips practically
invited the hijacking by ignoring safety protocols

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.100
and sailing too close to the coast.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:53.970
Phillips supposedly had multiple email warnings
about increasing piracy around Somalia.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:59.890
The crew also claims they endured two pirate
attacks on that trip, and not just the one.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.280
A Beautiful Mind

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:07.419
This Russell Crowe classic was an inspiring
look into the life story of economics Nobel

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:09.669
Laureate John Nash.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.669
Sort of.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:15.729
Nash never actually joined Wheeler Laboratory
at MIT...because it doesn't exist.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:20.909
And while the real Nash did become an instructor
at MIT, he never worked for the Department

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:21.909
of Defense.

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:26.819
The movie also shows him as a dad, and he
did become a father in real life…of a child

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.569
out of wedlock, and he abandoned the mother.

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:34.500
Nash also never actually gave an acceptance
speech of his Nobel prize.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.759
Still—it's not a bad way to end a movie.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:41.300
"The most important discovery of my life.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:49.030
It is only in the mysterious equations of
love that any logical reasons can be found."

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.080
That's the stuff that Oscar dreams are made
of...

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.509
"God bless and thank you for this support!"

00:06:55.509 --> 00:06:56.810
Thanks for watching!

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.949
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:05.759
And leave us a comment to let us know which
movie you can’t believe isn’t the whole

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:06.219
truth...

